== THE KILL RULE TRIPPED: what he decided on
Registered: decode at depth 2,048 or more under 0.98 on the KV5-2 build gets two tuning rounds within a working day; still under, KV5 parks.
1.5B: under at first (0.973, 0.968, 0.946); after round one 0.990, 0.990, 1.009. MC3 0.984.
gemma3-1b: under at first (0.969, 0.955); after round one 0.975, 0.963; after round two 0.975, 0.963. Both rounds are spent, and depth 3,900 reads FAIL.
gemma3-1b's geometry, from the checkpoint: 26 layers, 4 heads, 1 KV head, heads 256 wide, sliding window 512. It takes attention, since the fused kernels need heads of 128 or 64.
Not tuned, and not measured: round one's value pass tests two branches per 8-key tile for each head dimension, where the old kernel has none. The key pass's table load is about 1 in 64 of a key's reads at this width, so it is a weak candidate.
The cost grows with depth (at 128: 0.995 and 0.980), which fits a per-key or per-tile cost.
The rest of G2 is queued: docs/measurements/kv5-metal-2026-11/run-g2-night.sh, night job kv5-2-g2-7b.

